{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Java 8 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nVanya has a scales for weighing loads and weights of masses w0,\u2009w1,\u2009w2,\u2009...,\u2009w100 grams where w is some integer not less than 2 (exactly one weight of each nominal value). Vanya wonders whether he can weight an item with mass m using the given weights, if the weights can be put on both pans of the scales. Formally speaking, your task is to determine whether it is possible to place an item of mass m and some weights on the left pan of the scales, and some weights on the right pan of the scales so that the pans of the scales were in balance.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 8 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        new Main().mainInternal();\n    }\n\n    private void mainInternal() {\n        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n             PrintWriter writer = new PrintWriter(System.out)) {\n            String[] input = reader.readLine().trim().split(\"\\\\s+\");\n            long w = Long.parseLong(input[0]);\n            long m = Long.parseLong(input[1]);\n\n            if (m == 1) {\n                writer.println(\"YES\");\n                return;\n            }\n\n            Set<Long> states = new HashSet<>();\n            states.add(1L);\n            long weight = w;\n\n            for (int depth = 1; depth <= 60; depth++) {\n                Set<Long> nextStates = new HashSet<>(states);\n                for (long s : states) {\n                    nextStates.add(s + weight);\n                    nextStates.add(Math.abs(s - weight));\n                }\n                states = nextStates;\n                if (states.contains(m)) {\n                    writer.println(\"YES\");\n                    return;\n                }\n                if (weight > m * w) break;\n                weight *= w;\n            }\n\n            writer.println(\"NO\");\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            throw new RuntimeException(e);\n        }\n    }\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        new Main().mainInternal();\n    }\n\n    private void mainInternal() {\n        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n             PrintWriter writer = new PrintWriter(System.out)) {\n            String[] input = reader.readLine().trim().split(\"\\\\s+\");\n            long w = Long.parseLong(input[0]);\n            long m = Long.parseLong(input[1]);\n\n            // We can think of this as representing m in base w\n            // Each digit can be -1, 0, or 1 (representing putting weight on left pan, not using it, or putting weight on right pan)\n            // The item is on the left pan, so we need: m + (weights on left) = (weights on right)\n            // Which means: m = (weights on right) - (weights on left)\n            // So we need to represent m as sum of w^i * c_i where c_i \u2208 {-1, 0, 1}\n            \n            boolean possible = true;\n            while (m > 0) {\n                long remainder = m % w;\n                if (remainder == 0) {\n                    // No weight needed at this position\n                    m /= w;\n                } else if (remainder == 1) {\n                    // Put weight on right pan (positive contribution)\n                    m /= w;\n                } else if (remainder == w - 1) {\n                    // Put weight on left pan (negative contribution), which is equivalent to adding 1 to next higher power\n                    m = m / w + 1;\n                } else if (w - remainder == 1 && remainder > 1) {\n                    // This handles the case where we can use the weight on the opposite side\n                    // Actually, the general case: if remainder is not 0, 1, or w-1, it's impossible\n                    possible = false;\n                    break;\n                } else {\n                    // For other remainders, it's impossible to balance\n                    possible = false;\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n\n            writer.println(possible ? \"YES\" : \"NO\");\n        } catch (IOException e) {\n            throw new RuntimeException(e);\n        }\n    }\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.95\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-4.768370445162873e-07, -1.6689160474925302e-05, -2.622600959512056e-06, 0.0, -5.221230458118953e-05, -1.8358061424805783e-05, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -5.125986263010418e-06, -5.602820692729438e-06, -5.960446742392378e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -3.015949550899677e-05, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -3.576278118089249e-07, -5.364403477869928e-06, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -4.95898348162882e-05, -5.960462772236497e-07, -8.344646857949556e-07, -5.245195097813848e-06, -1.26